#source(254)A coefficient system $cal(F)$ on a $G$-simplicial complex $Delta$
gives rise to a chain complex#idx("chain", "complex") as follows (cf.
Section~@sec:g-simplicial-complexes):

#definition[
  We define the $n$th chain group of $Delta$ with coefficients in $cal(F)$,
  $C_n (Delta; cal(F))$#idx("$C_n (Delta; cal(F))$") to be the $k$-module
  generated by the $cal(F)(sigma)$ as $sigma$ ranges over the oriented
  $n$-simplices of $Delta$, with relators saying that each element of
  $cal(F)(sigma)$ is equal to minus the corresponding element of
  $cal(F)(sigma^op)$, where $sigma^op$ denotes the same simplex with the
  opposite orientation.

  The vector space $C_n (Delta; cal(F))$ inherits a $G$-module structure from
  the action of $G$ on $cal(F)$.
] <def:coefficient-chain-group>

Just as the chain groups $C_n (Delta; k)$ of Section~@sec:g-simplicial-complexes
are permutation modules, the modules $C_n (Delta; cal(F))$ are sums of modules
induced from stabilisers of $n$-simplices. The case of $C_n (Delta; k)$ is
simply the special case of the constant system with coefficients in the trivial
$k G$-module $k$.

The boundary map
$ delta_n : C_n (Delta; cal(F)) -> C_(n-1) (Delta; cal(F)) $
is defined analogously to the case of trivial coefficients, but using the
alternating sum of the restriction maps to faces of a simplex. If
$u in cal(F)(sigma)$ with $sigma = (x_0, dots, x_n)$ then
$ delta(u) = sum_(i=0)^n (-1)^i res_(sigma, sigma_i) (u) $
where $sigma_i = (x_0, dots, x_(i-1), x_(i+1), dots, x_n)$.

It is again easy to check that $delta_n compose delta_(n+1) = 0$, and that the
boundary maps are $k G$-module homomorphisms, so that the homology groups
$ H_n (Delta; cal(F)) = Ker(delta_n) slash Im(delta_(n+1)) $
#idx("$H_n (Delta; cal(F))$")are also $k G$-modules.

#exercise[
  Show that $H_0 (Delta; cal(F))$ is the same as the $k G$-module $H_0 (cal(F))$
  introduced in the last section. For this reason, we may use the notation
  $H_i (cal(F))$ for $H_i (Delta; cal(F))$ without ambiguity.
]

#example(kind: "Examples")[
  + (Solomon, Tits) If $Delta = |cal(B)_p (G)|$ with $G$ a Chevalley group in
    characteristic $p$ of semisimple rank $r >= 2$, then
    $
      H_i (kappa_M) = cases(
        M & quad i = 0,
        M times.o St & quad i = r - 1,
        0 & quad "otherwise."
      )
    $
  + (Lusztig [@bib:Lusztig1974]) If $G = SL_n (FF_q)$#idx("$SL_n (FF_q)$") with
    $n >= 3$ and $M$ is the natural $n$-dimensional module, then#ed-note[The
      book states that $H_i (cal(F)_M) = 0$ for all $i > 0$. The top homology
      does not vanish: for $G = SL_3 (FF_2)$ both chain groups have dimension
      21, so that $H_1 (cal(F)_M)$ has the dimension 3 of
      $H_0 (cal(F)_M) tilde.equiv M$; a direct computation for $n <= 5$ and
      small $q$ gives for $H_(n-2) (cal(F)_M)$ the dimension
      $(q - 1)(q^2 - 1) dots.c (q^(n-1) - 1)$. For $n = 2$, $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ has
      dimension $q + 1$.]
    $ H_i (cal(F)_M) = cases(M & quad i = 0, 0 & quad 0 < i < n - 2.) $
  + If $G = Sp_4 (FF_q)$#idx("$Sp_4 (FF_q)$") and $M$ is the natural
    4-dimensional module, then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(a)",
    [#source(255)if $p != 2$ then $H_0 (cal(F)_M) = M$;],
    [whereas if $p = 2$ then $dim H_0 (cal(F)_M) = 5$, and there is a non-split
      short exact sequence
      $ 0 -> k -> H_0 (cal(F)_M) -> M -> 0. $],
  )

  In fact $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ is the natural orthogonal module for
  $Sp_4 (FF_q) tilde.equiv O_5 (FF_q)$#idx("$Sp_4 (FF_q)$")#idx(
    "symplectic group",
    "$Sp_4 (FF_q)$",
  )#idx("group", "symplectic", "$Sp_4 (FF_q)$")#idx("$O_5 (FF_q)$")#idx(
    "orthogonal group",
    "$O_5 (FF_q)$",
  )#idx("group", "orthogonal", "$O_5 (FF_q)$") in this latter case. We shall
  have more to say about this example in the next section.
]

#subhead[Long exact sequences] Recall from Section~2.3 of Volume~I that a short
exact sequence of chain complexes $0 -> bold(C)' -> bold(C) -> bold(C)'' -> 0$
gives rise to a long exact sequence of homology groups
$
  dots.c -> H_i (bold(C)') -> H_i (bold(C)) -> H_i (bold(C)'')
  -> H_(i-1) (bold(C)') -> \
  dots.c -> H_0 (bold(C)) -> H_0 (bold(C)'') -> 0.
$

Thus if $0 -> cal(F) -> cal(G) -> cal(G) slash cal(F) -> 0$ is a short exact
sequence of coefficient systems, then there is a long exact sequence of homology
groups
$
  dots.c -> H_i (cal(F)) -> H_i (cal(G)) -> H_i (cal(G) slash cal(F))
  -> H_(i-1) (cal(F)) -> dots.c .
$

If you know about left derived functors, you will recognise that this is saying
that the $H_i$ are the left derived functors of $dirlim$. In a similar way, one
can construct right derived functors $H^i$ of $invlim$ using the Čech approach
to cohomology, via the covering given by the simplices. We shall not be using
$H^i$ in this context.

#example[
  From the short exact sequence
  $ 0 -> cal(F)_M -> kappa_M -> kappa_M slash cal(F)_M -> 0 $
  we obtain a long exact sequence which ends with
  $
    dots.c -> H_0 (cal(F)_M) -> H_0 (kappa_M) -> H_0 (kappa_M slash cal(F)_M)
    -> 0.
  $
  Since $H_0 (kappa_M) = M$, this says that the natural map
  $H_0 (cal(F)_M) -> M$ is surjective if and only if
  $H_0 (kappa_M slash cal(F)_M) = 0$. Under these conditions we say that $M$ is
  *generated by fixed points*#idx(
    "generating",
    "coefficient system",
    "by fixed points",
  ). This is always the case, for example, if $M$ is irreducible.
]
